 :hypothesis
 {:confidence :strong-code-supported
  :statement "The parent event loop cannot process its deadline or cancellation while synchronous acceptance runs. An isolated trusted validator lets that event loop enforce termination even when the same pure implementation is slow."
  :not-claimed "No fix, complete test gate, runtime benchmark victory, production artifact approval or real-time OS scheduling guarantee is demonstrated here."}
 :recommendation
 {:choice :fixed-trusted-cancellable-validator-process
  :smallest-defensible-path
  "Keep the pure acceptance laws unchanged. Add one fixed BB validation entrypoint and extend the existing NBB process supervisor to own both renderer and validator under ONE absolute deadline. Existing BB argv/config/group-management facilities make this less invasive than introducing a resumable parser/serializer/hash engine. BB is a capability choice, not a proven speed optimization: the supervisor must timeout slow validation. An explicitly pinned NBB process is an alternative if qualification measurements favor it, not an automatic fallback."
  :important-output-boundary
  "Do not return accepted artifacts as EDN and call frame/read-one on the parent again: that reintroduces the blocking parser. Have the trusted validator emit a narrowly specified JSON transport record of its already-validated Result. Native JSON.parse plus explicit bounded shallow reconstruction of at most 64 descriptors and 64 content strings avoids re-running the character-level EDN parser, canonicalizer or portable SHA on the event loop. JSON is private IPC, not a new public semantic authority or renderer protocol. Benchmark this final conversion at its full cap; check monotonic time immediately before and after it."
  :file-boundaries
  [{:file "scripts/validate-render.clj (new fixed file)"
    :job "Read bounded request and raw renderer-output bytes, decode UTF-8 strictly preserving BOM identity, parse request with frame/read-one, call generation/accept-response using separately supplied trusted pins, and emit only a closed JSON Result. No eval/load-file/request-selected code or files."
    :framing "Use two bounded decimal byte lengths in fixed trusted argv (request <=262144, renderer-output <=1048576) followed by exactly those two byte blocks on stdin and EOF; parse/check lengths before allocation. Pins are a separate bounded restricted-EDN argv value from trusted context, not read from renderer bytes. Parent invokes only after renderer exit 0, empty stderr BY BYTE COUNT, EOF/close and group reconciliation; otherwise use existing closed errors without validating success. Preserve the renderer frame's LF requirement and original bytes. Never use unbounded slurp, line splitting of arbitrary renderer EDN, or canonicalization of a huge envelope on the parent."
    :output "Built-in cheshire.core can emit JSON. Encode keyword-valued semantic fields explicitly (e.g. status, error code, format, validator-id), with a fixed reverse mapping in NBB; do not accidentally lose namespace in :oikos/profile-edn. Carry strings literally. Errors contain fixed codes/coordinates only. Unexpected validator exit, stderr, schema, overflow or truncation fails closed. Bound this private output independently with a documented conservative expansion allowance for JSON escaping; do not change the frozen renderer stdout cap."
    :trust "This entrypoint and its law files/config/executable are independently owner-approved immutable validator context. It must not be selected by a renderer response or inherit a mutable/untrusted renderer's helper path as authority."}
   {:file "src/oikos/infra/bb_render.cljs"
    :job "Replace synchronous close-handler acceptance with launching the fixed validator; retain the bounded raw request wire and renderer byte buffers until then. Factor the current child/group/pipe cleanup into per-process records, not a second independent public adapter."
    :state "Keep one terminal CAS and one monotonic deadline for the operation; track renderer and validator process records independently. All spawned owned groups participate in cleanup, with a single parallel grace/kill/cleanup budget, not 1000 ms per sequential process. Validation startup consumes the remaining original 5000 ms; never reset the clock after renderer close."
    :clock "Capture a monotonic timestamp at the actual renderer spawn boundary. Before launching validator, before consuming its result, and immediately before publishing artifacts: terminal must still be nil AND now < deadline. Otherwise timeout owns the decision. A delivered success event before an overdue timer callback is not permission to succeed. Retain the timer through validation."
    :cleanup "Cancel/timeout marks terminal before signals. Close each owned stdin, TERM each still-owned live group, KILL after 250 ms, and reconcile close/reaping/group absence by the common further 750 ms. Any unconfirmed group remains an explicit cleanup hold blocking admission. Never resurrect or signal a reconciled PID from a late callback. Clear raw buffers on settlement."
    :parent-work "Do not run accept-response, frame/read-one, canonical on the response, portable UTF-8 traversal, or portable hashing on the main loop after spawn. Native bounded Buffer copies/decode/JSON.parse and bounded shallow descriptor reconstruction remain; measure them. Return only data from the trusted validation process after its own close and cleanup proof."
    :pins "Extend the closed trusted descriptor/pin inventory deliberately for the extra fixed helper or use a separate closed validator descriptor. Snapshotting files in a writable candidate remains test convenience, NOT production TOCTOU protection. Keep NBB the light outer runtime and retain the allowlisted synthetic environment."}
   {:file "src/oikos/law/generation.cljc"
    :job "No acceptance-law rewrite required for this deadline repair. Keep full independent request/result validation in the validator process. Avoid coupling portable law to process clocks or cancellation."
    :separate-pre-spawn-concern "start!:98-99 already canonicalizes and fully renders synchronously before actual spawn (reported about 5 seconds in the probe). Moving only acceptance restores the specified spawn-relative terminal budget, not start!-entry responsiveness. Do not claim the latter. If also fixing preflight, extract non-rendering request/capability admission checks and move expensive full request-result work into the cancellable trusted execution boundary; retain unavailable/mixed-capability denial before renderer spawn. Do not silently delete independent source/request checks."}]
  :why-not-cooperative-now
  ["A Promise, queueMicrotask, setTimeout wrapping accept-response, or a yield between targets still blocks during parsing one near-1MiB response and hashing/canonicalizing a 64KiB artifact. Deadline checks without yields detect lateness but cannot deliver a terminal result during that blocking interval."
   "A real cooperative solution needs resumable frame parsing (including string scanning and UTF-8 checks), serializer, UTF-8 encoder and SHA blocks plus per-target validation, with bounded slices and event-loop yields across ALL paths. That is a multi-law/runtime change, not a small close-handler patch. An instruction/work budget alone needs a demonstrated hardware/time bound; element count does not bound long-string CPU time."
   "Do not put deadline side effects into portable laws merely to interrupt reductions; a thrown budget error cannot return control before its next checkpoint, and a same-loop cancellation callback still cannot run until a yield."
   "Off-thread execution retains current acceptance semantics and provides a forcible kill boundary. It does not require trusting the producer or claiming all valid near-cap workloads complete in five seconds. Valid but slow workloads may timeout under the unchanged wall budget; changing supported sizes or claiming positive large-case qualification requires separate evidence."]}
